{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And there was strife between the herdmen of Abram's cattle, etc.</b> Rashi explained the juxtaposition of 'and the Canaanite and the Perizzite were then dwelling in the land' to say that Abram had not yet merited it. And it is difficult: why did it mention here 'and the Perizzite,' whereas above (Genesis 12:6) it said 'and the Canaanite was then in the land,' and here it mentioned 'dwelling' and did not mention this above? And some say that it wished to say: these two great nations, the Canaanite and the Perizzite, settled in the land without strife, and these two herdsmen — the land could not bear them to dwell together. And it is further difficult: was it on account of this that he said 'Separate thy now, I pray thee, from me' (Genesis 13:9)? Behold, Abram's herdmen did what was theirs to do and rebuked them for the theft, and if they did not accept their reproof, behold they — Abram's herdmen — saved their own souls." "And it is more plausible to hear that Abram's herdmen rebuked them for the theft, and they — Lot's herdmen — claimed that the whole land belongs to Abram, etc., and Abram reasoned that if the inhabitants of the land see that he is seizing hold of the land with the presumption that it belongs to Abram while 'I am men of number,' plainly they will gather against me and strike me. Therefore he also mentioned the Perizzite, whose men were mighty to the degree that they dwelt in unwalled towns, as indicated by the name Perizzite, and as the sign that Moses gave to the spies. And so Jacob feared the Perizzite most of all, as he said (Genesis 34:30), 'Ye have troubled me, to make me stink among the inhabitants of the land, among the Canaanite and the Perizzite, and I am men of number,' etc. And therefore it mentioned here 'dwelling,' to say that until now they dwell in tranquility with me, but should they see that my herdmen seize hold of the fields with the presumption that they are mine, they will fight me for nothing. Therefore 'Separate thy now, I pray thee, from me.' Therefore Lot went and settled near the men of Sodom, who are not particular about theft. Therefore Scripture juxtaposed to say 'and the men of Sodom were wicked and sinners,' to indicate that because they were sinners in their money and were not particular about theft, Lot settled near them."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:11.257885243Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}